WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.343
Marcus has two quotes on his desk.

00:00:02.343 --> 00:00:06.695
The first is a traditional plan: he sees a doctor, the doctor bills

00:00:06.695 --> 00:00:10.712
for the service, and the plan reimburses a share of the charge.

00:00:10.712 --> 00:00:14.394
That is fee-for-service, also called indemnity: payment follows each service rendered.

00:00:14.394 --> 00:00:18.411
The second is a health maintenance organization: he pays a fixed monthly

00:00:18.411 --> 00:00:22.427
amount and the organization provides care through its own doctors and facilities.

00:00:22.427 --> 00:00:25.440
That is a prepaid basis: the money is paid

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.118
in advance for care, not after each service.

00:00:28.118 --> 00:00:31.800
Almost every other difference between plans grows out of this one.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.994
Older basic plans listed specified coverages: a hospital expense benefit

00:00:34.994 --> 00:00:38.189
for room and board, a surgical expense benefit paying by

00:00:38.189 --> 00:00:41.064
a schedule, a physician expense benefit for office visits.

00:00:41.064 --> 00:00:43.938
Each had its own limits and gaps between them.

00:00:43.938 --> 00:00:48.091
Comprehensive care, the modern major medical approach, covers the full range of hospital,

00:00:48.091 --> 00:00:51.924
surgical, physician, diagnostic and preventive services under one deductible and coinsurance structure.

00:00:51.924 --> 00:00:56.077
The Affordable Care Act requires individual and small group plans to cover ten

00:00:56.077 --> 00:00:59.910
essential health benefit categories, which pushed the market firmly toward comprehensive design.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:03.182
How much does the plan pay for an appendectomy?

00:01:03.182 --> 00:01:07.182
A benefit schedule names a flat dollar amount for each procedure,

00:01:07.182 --> 00:01:11.181
regardless of what the surgeon charges; the patient pays any excess.

00:01:11.181 --> 00:01:15.181
A usual, customary and reasonable approach pays based on the going

00:01:15.181 --> 00:01:19.181
rate: the fee the provider usually charges, what similar providers in

00:01:19.181 --> 00:01:23.180
the area customarily charge, and what is reasonable for the circumstances.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.907
Charges above UCR are the patient’s responsibility unless the provider is in network

00:01:27.907 --> 00:01:32.270
and has agreed to accept the plan’s allowance as payment in full.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.849
An indemnity plan lets Marcus see any licensed provider

00:01:35.849 --> 00:01:39.031
and pays the same share wherever he goes.

00:01:39.031 --> 00:01:43.008
A managed care plan limits choice to a network of

00:01:43.008 --> 00:01:46.986
providers who have agreed to discounted fees and plan rules.

00:01:46.986 --> 00:01:50.963
An HMO generally pays nothing outside the network except emergencies.

00:01:50.963 --> 00:01:54.542
A PPO pays more in network and less out.

00:01:54.542 --> 00:01:57.724
The trade-off is simple: limited choice buys lower

00:01:57.724 --> 00:02:00.906
premiums and negotiated prices; any-provider freedom costs more.

00:02:00.906 --> 00:02:04.883
The exam describes a plan’s rules and asks you to

00:02:04.883 --> 00:02:08.860
name it, so anchor each name to its provider rule.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.622
Vocabulary signals plan type.

00:02:10.622 --> 00:02:14.586
An insurance company issues a policy to an insured.

00:02:14.586 --> 00:02:19.431
A nonprofit health service plan or an HMO issues a subscriber

00:02:19.431 --> 00:02:23.835
contract or evidence of coverage to a subscriber or member.

00:02:23.835 --> 00:02:29.561
An employer’s self-funded plan covers participants under a plan document governed by ERISA.

00:02:29.561 --> 00:02:35.727
The words matter because the regulator, the guaranty protection and the contract documents differ.

00:02:35.727 --> 00:02:40.132
When Priya’s HMO quote calls her workers members and lists

00:02:40.132 --> 00:02:44.536
copayments instead of coinsurance, those words tell you it is

00:02:44.536 --> 00:02:48.500
a prepaid, limited-choice plan before you read the benefits.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.446
Describe any plan with the five pairs.

00:02:52.446 --> 00:02:58.083
Marcus’s indemnity quote: fee-for-service, comprehensive, UCR reimbursement, any provider, insured.

00:02:58.083 --> 00:03:02.593
The HMO quote: prepaid, comprehensive, fixed copayments instead

00:03:02.593 --> 00:03:07.102
of UCR, limited choice with a gatekeeper, member.

00:03:07.102 --> 00:03:13.867
A PPO sits between them: fee-for-service with negotiated network rates, comprehensive, in-network

00:03:13.867 --> 00:03:20.631
allowance accepted as payment in full, two levels of provider choice, insured.

00:03:20.631 --> 00:03:27.396
Lessons 19 through 22 fill in the deductibles, coinsurance, gatekeepers and networks.

00:03:27.396 --> 00:03:34.160
The concepts here are the grammar; the plan names are the vocabulary.
